          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described about salt and fluid for someone living with heart failure?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve does not answer your question. None of the documents covers salt or fluid intake for people with heart failure, and I have not filled the gap from memory.\n\n- **Hypertension guidance (Major cardiology society consensus guidance)** lists reducing dietary sodium as a shared lifestyle measure for high blood pressure. It does not address heart failure.\n- **Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction guidance (Major cardiology society consensus guidance)** covers four foundational medication classes. It does not mention diet, salt or fluid.\n\nBecause of these gaps, I can't say what current heart failure guidance recommends on salt or fluid limits, how strong the evidence is for any limit, or where authorities disagree. My broader search for trial evidence also failed to return results, so I can't confirm what studies show.\n\nSalt and fluid advice in heart failure is individual. The right amount depends on the type of heart failure, kidney function, medicines and symptoms. Ask the heart failure team or your clinician what limits apply to you, and ask whether a dietitian referral would help. If you notice sudden worsening breathlessness, chest pain, fainting, or rapid weight gain with swelling, seek urgent medical care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
